('raising-kids-who-own-their-screen-habits-small-changes-big-difference', 'Raising Kids Who Own Their Screen Habits: Small Changes, Big Difference', 'There''s a version of raising kids who own their screen habits that feels like a fight, and a version that feels like a rhythm. Below is a calm, practical way to think about it — no', 'The Bubaly Team', '2026-03-26', 3, ARRAY['#bubaly','#familylife','#parenting','#raisingkids','#organizedhome','#familyfirst']::text[], 'Parenting', false, '#7c5dff', null, null, null, $json$[{"type":"p","text":"There's a version of raising kids who own their screen habits that feels like a fight, and a version that feels like a rhythm. This is about getting to the second one."},{"type":"p","text":"Below is a calm, practical way to think about it — not a rigid program, just a handful of ideas you can borrow tonight and adjust for your own family."},{"type":"h2","text":"Praise the effort, not the trait"},{"type":"p","text":"\"You worked hard on that\" grows a child who tries; \"you're so smart\" grows one who fears being wrong. The words are small; the difference compounds for years."},{"type":"h2","text":"Let them be bored"},{"type":"p","text":"Boredom is the doorway to imagination. Resisting the urge to fill every gap teaches kids to generate their own fun — a skill worth more than any enrichment class."},{"type":"h2","text":"Give the choice, keep the frame"},{"type":"p","text":"\"Bath now or after this show?\" beats \"Go take a bath.\" The child gets agency; you keep the outcome. It's the oldest trick in the parenting book because it works."},{"type":"h2","text":"Lower the bar on purpose"},{"type":"p","text":"Perfectionism is the enemy of a warm home. A \"good enough\" dinner eaten together beats a Pinterest meal served to a stressed-out table."},{"type":"h2","text":"Protect the wind-down"},{"type":"p","text":"The hour before bed sets the tone for the night and the morning after. Dimmer lights, lower voices, and screens away make sleep arrive on its own instead of by force."},{"type":"h2","text":"A simple place to start"},{"type":"p","text":"First, decide the one default that removes a daily decision. Next, agree on who owns it so it isn't quietly one person's job. Then, give it two weeks before you judge whether it's working."},{"type":"h2","text":"Where Bubaly fits in"},{"type":"p","text":"Want raising kids who own their screen habits to run itself? That's what Bubaly does — one calm home for your family's schedule, tasks, meals, and money, with AI that stays a step ahead. Start at bubaly.com."},{"type":"p","text":"Start small, stay consistent, and let the system do the remembering so you don't have to."}]$json$::jsonb, true),
('a-simple-system-for-the-first-sleepover-ground-rules', 'A Simple System for The First Sleepover Ground Rules', 'The First Sleepover Ground Rules is one of those quietly relentless parts of family life: never urgent enough to fix, never small enough to ignore. Here''s the framework, the small ', 'Hannah Cho', '2026-04-17', 3, ARRAY['#bubaly','#familylife','#parenting','#firstsleepover','#dadlife','#familytime']::text[], 'Parenting', false, '#7c5dff', null, null, null, $json$[{"type":"p","text":"The First Sleepover Ground Rules is one of those quietly relentless parts of family life: never urgent enough to fix, never small enough to ignore."},{"type":"p","text":"Here's the framework, the small habits that make it stick, and the honest caveats nobody mentions."},{"type":"h2","text":"Consistency over intensity"},{"type":"p","text":"A quiet rule kept every day outperforms a dramatic one kept once. Kids are scientists; they run experiments on our boundaries, and steadiness gives them the answer faster than volume."},{"type":"h2","text":"Name the feeling before the fix"},{"type":"p","text":"Kids (and adults) calm down when they feel understood, not when they're handed a solution. A quick \"you really wanted more time, huh?\" lands better than a lecture. With the first sleepover ground rules, naming the emotion first buys you the cooperation the logic needs."},{"type":"h2","text":"Special time, protected"},{"type":"p","text":"Ten minutes of undivided, child-led attention a day does more for behavior than an hour of correction. Put it on the calendar so it survives busy weeks."},{"type":"h2","text":"Routines beat reminders"},{"type":"p","text":"A predictable sequence — same steps, same order — means you stop being the nag and the routine becomes the boss. Kids resist people; they rarely resist a chart they helped make."},{"type":"h2","text":"One-on-one beats the group"},{"type":"p","text":"Even a quick errand run solo with one child fills a cup that group time can't reach. Rotate who gets the passenger seat and watch the rivalry cool."},{"type":"h2","text":"A simple place to start"},{"type":"p","text":"First, start smaller than feels necessary — momentum beats ambition. Next, agree on who owns it so it isn't quietly one person's job. Then, write down the plan so it survives a chaotic day."},{"type":"h2","text":"Where Bubaly fits in"},{"type":"p","text":"If keeping the first sleepover ground rules on track sounds exhausting, that's the point where Bubaly quietly helps. It's the AI operating system for family life — it remembers, reminds, and coordinates so you don't have to carry it all alone. See how at bubaly.com."},{"type":"p","text":"Start small, stay consistent, and let the system do the remembering so you don't have to."}]$json$::jsonb, true),
('a-simple-system-for-helping-a-child-through-a-fear-of-dogs', 'A Simple System for Helping a Child Through a Fear of Dogs', 'Nobody puts helping a child through a fear of dogs on their vision board, and yet it shapes the temperature of the whole house. Here''s the framework, the small habits that make it ', 'Jessica Miller', '2026-05-07', 3, ARRAY['#bubaly','#familylife','#parenting','#helpingchild','#organizedhome','#familyfirst']::text[], 'Parenting', false, '#7c5dff', null, null, null, $json$[{"type":"p","text":"Nobody puts helping a child through a fear of dogs on their vision board, and yet it shapes the temperature of the whole house."},{"type":"p","text":"Here's the framework, the small habits that make it stick, and the honest caveats nobody mentions."},{"type":"h2","text":"One-on-one beats the group"},{"type":"p","text":"Even a quick errand run solo with one child fills a cup that group time can't reach. Rotate who gets the passenger seat and watch the rivalry cool."},{"type":"h2","text":"Lower the bar on purpose"},{"type":"p","text":"Perfectionism is the enemy of a warm home. A \"good enough\" dinner eaten together beats a Pinterest meal served to a stressed-out table."},{"type":"h2","text":"Repair is the real lesson"},{"type":"p","text":"You will lose your temper. The magic isn't never rupturing — it's the repair afterward. \"I was frustrated and I snapped; that wasn't fair\" teaches more about relationships than a hundred calm days."},{"type":"h2","text":"The 2-minute head start"},{"type":"p","text":"Transitions are where meltdowns live. A gentle countdown — \"two more minutes, then we clean up\" — respects a child's need to finish a thought and heads off half your battles."},{"type":"h2","text":"Special time, protected"},{"type":"p","text":"Ten minutes of undivided, child-led attention a day does more for behavior than an hour of correction. Put it on the calendar so it survives busy weeks."},{"type":"h2","text":"A simple place to start"},{"type":"p","